1. Introduction 
In the wake of the global COVID-19 pandemic, data-driven insights have become crucial 
for public health strategies and vaccine policy decisions. This project presents a 
comprehensive analysis of COVID-19 outcomes by vaccination status in the United 
States, using a cleaned and structured dataset sourced from public health records. The 
analysis was performed using Python and data visualization libraries such as Matplotlib 
and Seaborn. 
The primary objective is to explore the relationship between vaccination status and key 
COVID-19 outcomes such as cases, hospitalizations, and deaths. By examining trends 
across different age groups and time periods, the report aims to uncover patterns that 
inform the effectiveness of vaccination campaigns. The cleaned dataset, visualized 
through graphs and summary tables, provides a valuable tool for health analysts, 
researchers, and policy-makers to evaluate public health responses and plan for future 
contingencies. 
2. Source of Dataset 
The dataset used in this project comes from public health records and is titled COVID-19 
Outcomes by Vaccination Status - Historical. It includes information about COVID-19 
cases, hospitalizations, and deaths, grouped by vaccination status such as Unvaccinated, 
Vaccinated, and Boosted. The data also includes different age groups and is updated 
weekly. 
Since the dataset covers several months, it helps us see how outcomes have changed over 
time. This makes it useful for understanding how vaccines have helped reduce the impact 
of COVID-19. The dataset is a good source for analysing trends and making informed 
decisions in healthcare and public safety. 
